<h3 class=3D"post-title entry-title" itemprop=3D"name">
<a href=3D"http://fractalfinance.blogspot.com/2011/03/logic-of-place.html">=
The Logic of Place</a>
</h3>
<div class=3D"post-header">
<div class=3D"post-header-line-1"></div>
</div>
<div class=3D"post-body entry-content" id=3D"post-body-152729772421153838" =
itemprop=3D"description articleBody">
In this post, I will attempt to present succinctly the Logic of Place as cr=
eated and understood by Nishida Kitaro, and then to draw some of its implic=
ations.<br>  <br><br><span style=3D"font-weight:bold;">I- NISHIDA=E2=80=99S=
 LOGIC OF BASHO (=E5=A0=B4 =E6=89=80 =E3=81=AE=E8=AB=96 =E7=90=86 , BASHO N=
O RONRI)</span><br><br>Nishida=E2=80=99s Logic of Basho closely relates to =
his writing style and therefore to the Japanese language itself, as is show=
n by Jacynthe Tremblay in <a href=3D"http://nirc.nanzan-u.ac.jp/publication=
s/EJPhilosophy/PDF/EJP6%20Tremblay.pdf#search=3D'basho%20logic'">this artic=
le ([1])</a>. <br>From there, we may at once remark that an important featu=
re of this logic is its encompassing nature: a higher category (more univer=
sal) that is encompassing of lower one (more particular) is said to be its =
basho. This is illustrated, at the epistemological level, by the following:=
 <br> <br><span style=3D"font-weight:bold;">=E2=80=9CIn the judgment =E2=80=
=9Cred is a color,=E2=80=9D the copula (=E3=81=A7=E3=81=82=E3=82=8B) means =
at the objective level that the particular is located in the universal and =
that the latter becomes the basho of the former=E2=80=9D[1], p.257 </span><=
br><br>To the point that basho becomes embedded into each other:<br><br><sp=
an style=3D"font-weight:bold;">=E2=80=9CThe fact that in the strict sense, =
the basho is located in a basho means simply the consciousness.=E2=80=9D[1]=
,p.262, note 6</span><br><br>This embedding-into-each-others may even be an=
 embedding-in-itself, in the case of self-awareness:<br><br><span style=3D"=
font-weight:bold;">=E2=80=9CSelf-awareness means that the self sees itself =
in itself. Seeing without a seer means that the =E2=80=9Cself as noesis=E2=
=80=9D becomes the =E2=80=9Cself as basho,=E2=80=9D that is to say, the bas=
ho itself.=E2=80=9D[1],p.268</span><br><br>It would be a mistake however to=
 understand the basho in terms of Platonician forms, as is clearly stated i=
n note 12:<br><br><span style=3D"font-weight:bold;">=E2=80=9Cform and conte=
nt are given simultaneously=E2=80=9D[1],p.262, note 12</span><br><br>The id=
ea of simultaneity is particularly important here, it means that the basho =
and its content condition each other in a non-causal manner, which relates =
to the Buddhist concept of dependent origination. <br>In fact, the logic of=
 basho is a formalization of the classic Buddhist logic that is found in Na=
garjuna and his followers, an attempt to overcome its apparent contradictor=
y features such as the one expressed here in <a href=3D"http://www.erudit.o=
rg/revue/theologi/1996/v4/n2/602440ar.html?vue=3Dresume">another article by=
 Jacynthe Tremblay ([2])</a>(click on the PDF link on the left column to ac=
cess the full article):<br><br><span style=3D"font-weight:bold;">=E2=80=9CL=
a logique paradoxale de Nishida pr=C3=A9sente le n=C3=A9ant absolu comme la=
 matrice logique de la  d=C3=A9termination mutuelle des couples oppos=C3=A9=
s. Il permet la n=C3=A9antisation d'un terme en  auto-identit=C3=A9 pour qu=
'apparaisse son contraire, et vice versa. Comprise =C3=A0 partir du point d=
e vue du  n=C3=A9ant absolu, l'auto-identit=C3=A9 absolument contradictoire=
 de Nishida n'est  rien d'autre que la reprise philosophique rigoureuse de =
la logique bouddhiste qui  =C3=A9nonce que A=3DA; A=3Dnon-A; donc A=3DA. Le=
 terme =C2=ABauto-identit=C3=A9=C2=BB (jiko d=C3=B4itsu) correspond =C3=A0 =
=C2=ABA=3DA=C2=BB; les mots =C2=ABabsolument contradictoire=C2=BB (zettai m=
ujun teki) correspondent =C3=A0 =C2=ABA=3Dnon-A=C2=BB (ou A n'est pas A mai=
s devient  B ), c'est-=C3=A0-dire =C3=A0 la  n=C3=A9antisation de A par l'i=
nterm=C3=A9diaire du  n=C3=A9ant absolu.=E2=80=9D[2],p.70</span><br>  <br>W=
hich, in english, roughly gives:<br><br><span style=3D"font-style:italic;">=
=E2=80=9CThe paradoxical logic of Nishida presents absolute nothingness as =
the logical matrix of the reciprocal determination of opposite couples. It =
allows the nihilation of one term into self-identity for its opposite to ap=
pear, the absolutely contradictory self-identity of Nishida is nothing more=
 than the philosophical and rigorous translation of the Buddhist logic that=
 states: A=3DA; A=3Dnot-A; therefore A=3DA. The term =C2=ABself-identity=C2=
=BB (jiko d=C3=B4itsu) relates to =C2=ABA=3DA=C2=BB; the words =C2=ABabsolu=
tely contradictory=C2=BB (zettai mujun teki) relate to =C2=ABA=3Dnot-A=C2=
=BB (where A is not A but becomes B ), that is, the nihilation of A by mean=
s of absolute nothingness.=E2=80=9D</span><br><br> I don=E2=80=99t totally =
agree with the above remark, it is not the =E2=80=9Cthe absolutely contradi=
ctory self-identity=E2=80=9D that is =E2=80=9Cthe philosophical and rigorou=
s translation of the Buddhist logic=E2=80=9D, but the whole idea of a basho=
 logic, and it is where we can clearly see the difference between this logi=
c and the classic circular western logic. A bit earlier, Jacynthe Tremblay =
writes the following:<br><br><span style=3D"font-weight:bold;">=E2=80=9CEll=
e n'est pas bas=C3=A9e simplement sur une n=C3=A9gation, mais sur une n=C3=
=A9gation de la n=C3=A9gation, sur une n=C3=A9gation absolue qui n'est rien=
 d'autre qu'une affirmation absolue.=E2=80=9D[2],p.68</span><br><br><span s=
tyle=3D"font-style:italic;">=E2=80=9CIt[Nagarjuna=E2=80=99s Way of the Midd=
le] is not simply based on a negation, but on a negation of a negation, on =
an absolute negation that is nothing else than an absolute affirmation.=E2=
=80=9D</span><br><br>I think it would be a mistake to interpret this last s=
entence as being equivalent to the western logic proposition: not-not-A=3DA=
.<br>What Nagarjuna says here and what Nishida tries to clarify with his lo=
gic of basho is that A, via this process of double-negation is altering its=
elf, it is actually becoming its own basho by overcoming the duality that c=
onditions its very existence. <br>If now, we denote =E2=80=98a=E2=80=99 an =
entity and =E2=80=98A=E2=80=99 the basho within which this entity is locate=
d, we should then write: not-not-a=3DA<br>And possibly: A=3Dnot-A if A is t=
he basho of absolute nothingness, that obviously leads to the constitution =
of the self-aware subject as an =E2=80=9Cabsolutely contradictory self-iden=
tity=E2=80=9D.<br><br>A last point in this short presentation of Nishida=E2=
=80=99s logic is to notice its acquaintance with some of the points Meillas=
soux is making about the law of non-contradiction and the necessity of cont=
ingency. The logic of basho is ultimately a logic of becoming, its affirmat=
ion of absolute contradiction is really one that is meant to refine the eli=
minative Hegelian logic as is correctly pointed out by Robert E. Carter ([2=
],pp69-70) in order to preserve intact the dual tension between contradicti=
ng poles; such a move was also made, at about the same time, by Mao in his =
interpretation of Marx, he was then inspired, maybe subconsciously, by Chin=
ese classical philosophy. <br>When Meillassoux asserts the law of non-contr=
adiction as a corollary to the necessity of contingency, he is stating that=
 =E2=80=9Cbecoming=E2=80=9D is only possible as the result of a tension bet=
ween contradicting poles, as such he wrote:<br><br><span style=3D"font-weig=
ht:bold;">=E2=80=9CAffirmer qu=E2=80=99un existant peut ne plus exister, af=
firmer que cette possibilit=C3=A9, de surcro=C3=AEt, est quant =C3=A0 elle =
une n=C3=A9cessit=C3=A9 ontologique, c=E2=80=99est aussi bien affirmer que =
l=E2=80=99existence en g=C3=A9n=C3=A9ral de l=E2=80=99existant, au m=C3=AAm=
e titre que l=E2=80=99inexistence en g=C3=A9n=C3=A9ral de l=E2=80=99inexist=
ant sont les deux p=C3=B4les indestructibles par lesquels la destructibilit=
=C3=A9 de toute chose peut =C3=AAtre pens=C3=A9e.=E2=80=9D[MEI06], p.102</s=
pan><br><br><span style=3D"font-style:italic;">=E2=80=9CTo affirm that an e=
xistent can stop existing, to affirm that such a possibility is furthermore=
 an ontological necessity, that is also to affirm that the existence in gen=
eral of the existent, as well as the inexistence in general of the inexiste=
nt are the two indestructible poles by which the destructibility of all thi=
ngs may be thought.=E2=80=9D</span><br><br><br><span style=3D"font-weight:b=
old;">II- NISHIDA=E2=80=99S BASHO AND LEVINAS=E2=80=99 ILLEITY</span>   <br=
><br>In the note 28 of the second article, Jacynthe Tremblay writes the fol=
lowing:<br><br><span style=3D"font-weight:bold;">=C2=AB La relation je-tu (=
watashi to nanji) de Nishida se rapproche beaucoup de la relation =C3=8Ech-=
Du (ware to nanji) de Martin Buber. Nishida est en effet entr=C3=A9 en cont=
act avec la pens=C3=A9e de Buber sur cette question par l'interm=C3=A9diair=
e de la th=C3=A9ologie dialectique de Gogarten, entre autres. =C2=BB[2], p,=
73, note 28 </span><br> <br><span style=3D"font-style:italic;">=E2=80=9CThe=
 relation I-thou (watashi to nanji) from Nishida has a strong acquaintance =
with the relation =C3=8Ech-Du (ware to nanji) from Martin Buber. Nishida ha=
s indeed had contact with Buber=E2=80=99s thought on that question by means=
 of the dialectical theology of Gorgarten, among others.=E2=80=9D</span><br=
><br>This is correct to an extent, however, a better comparison would have =
been, in my view, with Levinas thought.  <br>Whereas Buber insists on <span=
 style=3D"font-weight:bold;">=E2=80=9Ca mutuality of relation that will eve=
ntually elide the difference between the I and thou=E2=80=9D</span>(<a href=
=3D"http://dlibrary.acu.edu.au/staffhome/dacasey/Levinas%20and%20Buber.html=
">[3]</a>), Levinas seems keener to =E2=80=9Cpreserve the <span style=3D"fo=
nt-weight:bold;">"reality of the difference between the `I' and `thou'"=E2=
=80=9D</span>([3]).  As such, Levinas is more in tune with the spirit of ni=
shida=E2=80=99s logic where nihilation is never to be taken as a cancellati=
on or an overcoming of the difference, but as a keeping intact of the tensi=
on of relation that founds the subject as an =E2=80=9Cabsolutely contradict=
ory self-identity=E2=80=9D( =E7=B5=B6 =E5=AF=BE =E7=9F=9B =E7=9B=BE =E7=9A=
=84 =E8=87=AA =E5=B7=B1 =E5=90=8C =E4=B8=80 : the aggregative nature of Jap=
anese language allows to reflect the dynamics at play, which would be destr=
oyed by a reconciliation of opposites).<br>Buber, therefore, does remain in=
to an Hegelian famework (via Feuerbach), and by insisting on reciprocity, h=
e precludes the field, the basho, within which the dynamics of becoming is =
to take place.<br>However, Levinas significantly deviates from Nishida when=
 he introduces a third party in the relation between I and thou, and this t=
hird party is God whose being-in-the world is the Illeity:<br><br><span sty=
le=3D"font-weight:bold;">=E2=80=9CIlleity lies outside the "thou" and the t=
hematization of objects. A neologism formed with il (he) or ille, it indica=
tes a way of concerning me without entering into conjunction with me. To be=
 sure, we have to indicate the element in which this concerning occurs.=E2=
=80=9D[3]</span><br><br>At first sight, =E2=80=9CIlleity=E2=80=9D may seem =
a concept equivalent to Nishida=E2=80=99s basho, if, we, for a minute, over=
look the usage of the word =E2=80=9CGod=E2=80=9D within the context of Juda=
ism, we may assume that, indeed, =E2=80=9CIlleity=E2=80=9D just provides a =
ground, a location for the relation to take place, especially in view of th=
e following remark:<br><br><span style=3D"font-weight:bold;">=E2=80=9CGod i=
s the absent condition of the encounter with the other.=E2=80=9D[3]</span><=
br><br>There is however a fundamental difference between Levinas=E2=80=99 I=
lleity and Nishida=E2=80=99s basho, that actually reflects the difference b=
etween the western monotheistic view of reality and  the eastern atheistic =
view of it. The absence of God, that structures its illeity is not the abso=
lute nothingness of the Nishida=E2=80=99s ultimate basho. Whereas the forme=
r posits a totalized world where God (even as the =E2=80=9CAbsent=E2=80=9D)=
 in its illeity appears as the fabric of relationality, by which <span styl=
e=3D"font-weight:bold;">=E2=80=9Can order is signified to me=E2=80=9D</span=
>([3], note 40), the latter posits the untotalizable emptiness of absolute =
nothingness. <br>Thereby, positing such a fabric is for Levinas to posit an=
 existing and eternal connectedness between I and thou, on the other hand, =
Nishida clearly affirms an impassable chasm between I and thou, a discontin=
uity that cannot be amended:<br><br><span style=3D"font-weight:bold;">=E2=
=80=9CLe lieu o=C3=B9 le je et le tu se situent et qui entra=C3=AEne l'auto=
-n=C3=A9gation de chacun d'entre eux, c'est-=C3=A0-dire le basho du n=C3=A9=
ant absolu, est l'interm=C3=A9diaire o=C3=B9 s'unit ce qui ne s'unit absolu=
ment pas, l'interm=C3=A9diaire o=C3=B9 le caract=C3=A8re absolu de chaque =
=C3=A9l=C3=A9ment et l'aspect d'absolue confrontation s'unissent dynamiquem=
ent. Pareille n=C3=A9gation ouvre dans le basho du n=C3=A9ant absolu un int=
ervalle insondable qui est la condition m=C3=AAme de la subjectivit=C3=A9 (=
shutaisei) du je et du tu. C'est l=C3=A0 une mani=C3=A8re de se joindre en =
se coupant, c'est-=C3=A0-dire en ne se touchant pas directement. Cela s'ass=
ocie directement au fait que le soi est le soi sans =C3=AAtre le soi, c'est=
-=C3=A0-dire comporte une interruption en lui-m=C3=AAme. Nishida mentionne =
que =C2=AB le je est le je par le fait de reconna=C3=AEtre la personnalit=
=C3=A9 du tu, et le tu est le tu par le fait de reconna=C3=AEtre la personn=
alit=C3=A9 du je. Ce qui fait du tu le tu est le je, et ce qui fait du je l=
e je est le tu. Le je et le tu =C3=A9tant une discontinuit=C3=A9 absolue, l=
e je d=C3=A9termine le tu et le tu d=C3=A9termine le je.=C2=BB=E2=80=9D[2]p=
.74</span><br><br><span style=3D"font-style:italic;">=E2=80=9CThe place whe=
re the I and the thou are located and which triggers the self-negation of e=
ach of them, that is, the basho of absolute nothingness, is the intermediar=
y where is united what is absolutely not united, the intermediary where the=
 absolute character of each element and the aspect of absolute confrontatio=
n get unified dynamically. Such a negation opens up in the absolute nothing=
ness an inscrutable interval that is the very condition of subjectivity (sh=
utaisei) of the I and the thou. This is a way to get united while being cut=
 off, that is without being in a direct contact of each others. That is dir=
ectly linked to the fact that oneself is oneself without being oneself, tha=
t there is a gap within oneself. Nishida mentions that =C2=AB the I is the =
I by the fact of recognizing the personality of the thou, and the thou is t=
he thou by the fact of recognizing the personality of the I. That which mak=
es the thou the thou is the I, and that which makes the I the I is the thou=
. The I and the thou being an absolute discontinuity, the I determines the =
thou, and thou determines the I.=C2=BB=E2=80=9D</span><br> <br>In conclusio=
n, whereas Levinas=E2=80=99 Illeity and Nishida=E2=80=99s basho do seem to =
fulfill a similar formal role in the constitution of relationality, Levinas=
=E2=80=99 Illeity does define a continuum, an ether within which the meetin=
g is meant to take place and to successfully be the foundation of ethics, o=
n the other hand, Nishida=E2=80=99s basho of absolute nothingness defines a=
n absolute disconnectness, a discontinuum, an empty place within which the =
meeting can never settled in anything but a tense and endless dynamics. <br=
>Not that Nishida=E2=80=99s view, or the way I understand it, cannot be use=
d to reveal an ethics, but not as directly as the way Levinas is proposing,=
 and not either as Watsuji does propose ([2],pp.74-79); I shall not, howeve=
r, discuss this ethical dimension here.<br><br><br><span style=3D"font-weig=
ht:bold;">III- THE BASHO OF THE MARKET</span>   <br><br>On page 442 of [EA1=
0], Ayache writes :<br><br><span style=3D"font-weight:bold;">=E2=80=9CMatte=
r is said to determine the geometry of space, even to preside over the gene=
sis of space, in Einstein=E2=80=99s general theory of relativity. Now what =
I am saying is that contingency (the only true and =E2=80=98original=E2=80=
=99 matter there really is in a materialistic ontology like Meillassoux=E2=
=80=99s) determines place.=E2=80=9D</span><br><br>As we have indeed seen ab=
ove, the logic of basho is really a logic of becoming, and. as such, it is =
easy to see that the basho of absolute nothingness is corollary to the nece=
ssity of contingency. It can also be noticed that price is the result of a =
meeting of offer and demand, and this meeting takes place in a market which=
 is otherwise empty. <br><br>It must then be clear that, if we are to follo=
w Nishida=E2=80=99s logic of basho and apply it to the dynamics of price, t=
his dynamics is infinite, discontinuous and untotalizable. Nothing new in t=
here really, the discontinuity of price time-series is an obvious thing, ev=
en though continuity is often preferred in terms of modelization, for the s=
ake of simplicity in getting mathematical results. The infinity goes withou=
t saying, and the untotalizability is already well-argued for in [EA10]. <b=
r>However, what the comparison between Nishida and Levinas is telling us is=
 that disconnectedness (I will stick to this word, from now on, as, I belie=
ve, it is more precise than discontinuity, to justify this choice would how=
ever take me too far here) is due, not to the process of price determinatio=
n, but to the basho; in other terms, the disconnectedness of price is a top=
ological property of the market-place and not a feature of price time-serie=
s.<br>This is a very important result since it invalidates the distinction =
between discrete and continuous models, as such models only address the pri=
ce process and do not account for the topology of the market place, which i=
s properly the determining factor in this regard.<br><br>Once again, such a=
 conclusion is only valid insofar that my understanding of Nishida=E2=80=99=
s point is valuable (i.e. productive), anyway, this is the direction of my =
reflection until I know better.      <br><br><br><br>            <br>Notes:=
<br><a href=3D"http://nirc.nanzan-u.ac.jp/publications/EJPhilosophy/PDF/EJP=
6%20Tremblay.pdf#search=3D'basho%20logic'">[1]: Nishida Kitar=C5=8D=E2=80=
=99s Language and Structure of Thought in the =E2=80=9CLogic of Basho=E2=80=
=9D - Jacynthe Tremblay</a> <br><a href=3D"http://www.erudit.org/revue/theo=
logi/1996/v4/n2/602440ar.html?vue=3Dresume">[2]: N=C3=A9antisation et relat=
ionalit=C3=A9 chez NISHIDA Kitar=C3=B4 et WATSUJI Tetsur=C3=B4 - Jacynthe T=
remblay</a>(click on the PDF link on the left column to access the full art=
icle)<br><a href=3D"http://dlibrary.acu.edu.au/staffhome/dacasey/Levinas%20=
and%20Buber.html">[3]: Levinas and Buber:Transcendence and Society - Damien=
 Casey</a>
